# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database access. Required by: doctor, install, status, sync, hook enable.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PostgreSQL connection string for your Supabase project.
# Supabase Dashboard -> Project Settings -> Database -> Connection string (URI).
#
# Keep `sslmode=require` (or stricter) so the connection is encrypted.
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill it in. Never commit `.env`.
SUPABASE_DB_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase Management API. Required by: hook enable, hook disable, hook status.
# Optional for: status (it reports remote activation only when both are set).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Your project reference: the 20-character id in your project's dashboard URL,
# https://supabase.com/dashboard/project/<THIS PART>
#
# Not a secret. It is validated before it is used in an API path.
SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF=

# Supabase Management API access token.
#
#   *** TREAT THIS AS THE MOST SENSITIVE VALUE IN THIS FILE. ***
#
# A personal access token carries the privileges of YOUR ACCOUNT across EVERY
# project that account can reach -- far wider than SUPABASE_DB_URL, which is
# scoped to one database. Anyone holding it can read and rewrite your projects'
# configuration.
#
# Create one at https://supabase.com/dashboard/account/tokens
# Prefer a fine-grained or OAuth token scoped to Auth configuration where your
# plan offers one, rather than a full-privilege personal access token.
#
# This CLI sends it only as an `Authorization: Bearer` header to
# https://api.supabase.com, never in a URL, never to a host you can configure,
# never to a log, and never to disk.
#
# Do NOT paste it into a shell command:
#
#     SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN=sbp_... npx supabase-anti-disposable-auth hook enable
#
# would write it into ~/.zsh_history or ~/.bash_history in plaintext, and into
# the process list where any other user on the machine can read it. Put it in
# this file instead (`.env` is gitignored), or export it from a secret manager:
#
#     export SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN="$(op read op://vault/supabase/token)"
#
# In CI, use the platform's masked-secret store. Never echo it.
SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Integration tests only. Optional.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scratch PostgreSQL database for `npm run test:integration`.
#
# WARNING: the guard-schema tests run `drop schema if exists guard cascade`
# against this database. Point it at a throwaway local database, never at a
# Supabase project you care about. This is intentionally NOT SUPABASE_DB_URL.
#
#   createdb supabase_anti_disposable_auth_test
#   SADA_TEST_DB_URL=postgresql://localhost:5432/supabase_anti_disposable_auth_test
SADA_TEST_DB_URL=

# Opt-in, READ-ONLY live checks against the Supabase Management API.
#
# Both must be set or the tests skip themselves. Deliberately NOT the same
# variables the CLI uses, for the same reason SADA_TEST_DB_URL is not
# SUPABASE_DB_URL: a test run must never reach for your real credentials by
# accident. The live tests only GET the Auth configuration -- they change
# nothing.
SADA_TEST_SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF=
SADA_TEST_SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# DANGEROUS OPT-IN, reserved. Gate for live MUTATION tests against a hosted project.
# No such test ships in 1.0; every state transition is covered against a mocked API
# instead, and this variable currently only makes the read-only suite assert that it
# changed nothing. Credentials being present is never permission to change an Auth
# configuration.
SADA_ALLOW_REMOTE_MUTATION_TESTS=
